{
  "id": "topics",
  "title": "Topics",
  "group": "Yon server",
  "summary": "A topic is a model marked `Topic` that carries events between Yon processes, languages and open pages (ADR 0061). When a Yon class publishes a Topic model, Tachyon adds it as one line to the log `data/topics/<name>.jsonl` and calls the `OnEvent` listeners of that model in the other Yon files and languages once. In a page, an `OnEvent` listener for the model opens the stream `/.tachyon/topics/<name>` by itself, in every Tac language; no `HTTPClient` is needed. The stream replays the kept records, and after a lost connection it resumes after the last record it received, through `Last-Event-ID`. A client may also ask for `?position=<n>`; a position the server no longer keeps ends the stream with `TY_TOPIC_CURSOR_STALE`. A server Topic model says who may read its stream, with the same access markers as a controller: `Public`, `SignedIn` or `Allow(Role)`, in the spelling of its language, next to `Topic`. A server Topic without one stops the build with `TY1322`, `SignedIn` or `Allow` without an identity provider stops it with `TY1323`, and every language that declares the same topic writes the same rule. A client topic model takes no access marker (`TY1308`): a page cannot grant itself access. The server answers 404 for a topic that no server model declares, and checks the rule before it opens the stream: 401 when nobody is signed in, and 403 when the user has none of the roles. A page opens its stream with its cookies, so an identity provider that reads a session cookie guards it. A record holds at most 64 KiB, a log at most 16 MiB, and one client address keeps at most 16 topic streams open. The examples are those of the Topics guide, where `InventoryRecord` is `Public`.",
